This project is to create a christmas star box to stay in the top of a chistmas tree. The star is made of three parts: The front and back (christmas star shaped) that snap together to close the box, and a conic base to lock the parts. PLan it before doing about choosing technology, and etc. The final asset is an STL to 3D print.

I'll plan this out — proposing technology and design approach, then a few sizing questions before generating STLs.


12:59 PM
Plan — Christmas star tree-topper box

Technology: OpenSCAD (parametric, scriptable, exports STL directly from CLI, easy to tweak dimensions later). I'd install it locally, write a single parametric .scad file, and render 3 STLs. Alternative would be CadQuery (Python), but OpenSCAD is simpler and more shareable for this kind of part.

Design:

Star shape: 5-pointed star outline (2D polygon), extruded. Front and back are each a hollow half-shell (star-shaped tray) so together they form a closed box.
Snap fit: the back half gets an inner lip/rim that slides into the front half, with small snap bumps + matching recesses around the rim (with printable clearance ~0.2 mm).
Conic base: a hollow cone at the bottom point of the star that slides over the tree tip; it caps over the joined bottom star point and locks the two halves together (friction/twist fit over both shells).
Print-friendly: flat outer faces so front/back print face-down without supports; cone prints wide-end down.
Deliverables: star_box.scad + 3 STLs (front, back, base), in a PR to a repo of your choice (or just attached files).

Default sizes I'd use unless you say otherwise: star ~150 mm tip-to-tip, ~40 mm deep, cone fits a tree tip up to ~25 mm diameter. OK to proceed with this plan?


